WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.347
A content delivery network places caching
proxies at multiple network locations. A nearby

00:00:05.347 --> 00:00:10.454
edge can answer an eligible repeated read
without contacting the origin that owns the

00:00:10.454 --> 00:00:15.020
original content. Consider an immutable image
identified by a versioned URL.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:19.901
The first edge request misses, fetches the image
from origin, and stores a reusable

00:00:19.901 --> 00:00:25.135
response; later requests use that copy until its
policy requires something different. The

00:00:25.135 --> 00:00:30.427
diagram separates edge storage from origin
ownership. This can reduce both origin work and

00:00:30.427 --> 00:00:32.956
the distance travelled for popular content.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:38.397
A private account page is different: proximity
does not make its contents safe to share, and

00:00:38.397 --> 00:00:41.085
its cache policy must reflect that difference.

00:00:41.085 --> 00:00:46.157
Anycast solves a traffic-steering problem rather
than a content-reuse problem. Multiple

00:00:46.157 --> 00:00:51.346
network locations advertise reachability for the
same address, and routing chooses a path

00:00:51.346 --> 00:00:56.593
according to topology and policy. The
illustration shows one destination address
reachable

00:00:56.593 --> 00:01:01.664
through two sites, with the selected network
path deciding which site receives traffic.

00:01:01.664 --> 00:01:07.155
This does not guarantee the geographically
closest server or automatically make either site

00:01:07.155 --> 00:01:12.344
a cache. A CDN may combine anycast, DNS
steering, and caching because those mechanisms

00:01:12.344 --> 00:01:17.593
answer different questions: where traffic goes,
how a client discovers an endpoint, and

00:01:17.593 --> 00:01:20.549
whether a saved response may replace origin
work.

00:01:20.664 --> 00:01:25.655
Keeping these roles separate prevents misleading
claims about what any one layer guarantees.

00:01:25.664 --> 00:01:30.636
Design a digital archive for the day a popular
collection is released. Public scans use

00:01:30.636 --> 00:01:35.779
immutable versioned URLs, so an edge cache can
answer repeated reads. Reservations for the

00:01:35.779 --> 00:01:40.808
final research-room seat follow a different
route: they must reach application logic and

00:01:40.808 --> 00:01:43.837
authoritative storage that enforces the booking
rule.

00:01:43.837 --> 00:01:48.924
The diagram deliberately branches after the
entry point rather than sending every operation

00:01:48.924 --> 00:01:53.732
through the same cache. Explain the cache key
and eligibility of the read branch, then

00:01:53.732 --> 00:01:57.421
explain authorization and atomic state change on
the write branch.

00:01:57.421 --> 00:02:02.072
A convincing architecture assigns each layer a
responsibility instead of assuming that

00:02:02.072 --> 00:02:06.021
adding more routing boxes automatically improves
correctness or capacity.

00:02:06.021 --> 00:02:11.380
Now fail one worker while a reservation response
is on its way back. The client times out

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:16.919
and repeats the request, but the original write
may already exist. At the same time, another

00:02:16.919 --> 00:02:19.147
user tries to reserve the final seat.

00:02:19.147 --> 00:02:24.243
The diagram shows the retry converging on the
original operation record while the competing

00:02:24.243 --> 00:02:28.892
reservation meets the same capacity constraint.
These are two different correctness

00:02:28.892 --> 00:02:33.988
concerns: deduplicating one user's repeated
operation and preventing two users from winning

00:02:33.988 --> 00:02:34.772
the same seat.

00:02:34.772 --> 00:02:39.963
A load balancer can select another healthy
worker, but the data and operation contracts
must

00:02:39.963 --> 00:02:44.985
make that replacement safe. Describe the
uncertain outcome explicitly before claiming the

00:02:44.985 --> 00:02:46.170
system has recovered.
